      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for  $f(x)={x}^{8}-{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8}-{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{(8-1)}-3{x}^{(3-1)}+(-2){x}^{-2-1} \\\\  & =8{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =8{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
